<?xml version="1.0" encoding="windows-1252"?>
<Module xmlns="http://waters.sourceforge.net/xsd/module" xmlns:B="http://waters.sourceforge.net/xsd/base" Name="EFSM Model">
<B:Comment>Changed state_num and state_numTEMP to be 0..9

Moved the require checks to teh initial events. This blocks in move(), since value is now assigned after the check, so teh check is done on the previously chosen value.

Note that we cannot have "state_whoseTurn == player2" as marking predicate for state_whoseTurn, there must be a specific value from the domain of state_whoseTurn.
The problem with this is that with three possible addresses we cannot know beforehand which address player2 will be assigned.
So the marking predicate could maybe be "state_whoseTurn == x0002 | state_whoseTurn == x0003". This could maybe work here, but in general...?

If player1 joins, and thsu plays against him/herself, this will eventually lead to blocking since the marking predicate of state_whoseTurn cannot be true. 
Synthesizing with this blocking state will lead to an empty supervisor, even if player1's moves are controllable.

Trying with the require checks on teh next-state values instead of current state. This seems to work...
We get two branches, one for when player2 == x0002, and one for when player2 == x0003; that is as expected.
Both branches allow player2 to do whatever move it wants, while player1 steers the game towards the win.</B:Comment>
